  "@@locale": "en",
  "baccaratRulesSummary": "Punto Banco, 8-deck shoe (416 cards), cut 16 cards from the end. On the first coup of every shoe, the first card is shown and as many cards as its value are burned (ace 1, tens and faces 10): the burn is part of the same fairness proof as the coup.",
  "baccaratRulesValues": "Card values: ace 1, 2 to 9 face value, tens and faces 0. A hand is worth the sum of its cards modulo 10 (7 + 8 = 5). Cards are dealt Player, Banker, Player, Banker. An 8 or 9 with two cards is a \"natural\": nobody draws.",
  "baccaratRulesDrawing": "Official drawing chart. Player draws on 0 to 5 and stands on 6 or 7. If Player stands, Banker draws on 0 to 5. If Player drew a third card (value p): Banker always draws on 0 to 2; on 3 unless p = 8; on 4 if p is 2 to 7; on 5 if p is 4 to 7; on 6 if p is 6 or 7; stands on 7. The third card is laid sideways.",
  "baccaratRulesPayouts": "Player 1:1 · Banker 0.95:1 (5% commission, exact to the cent) · Tie 8:1, and on a tie the Player and Banker bets are returned · Player Pair, Banker Pair 11:1 (first two cards of the same rank: K-Q is not a pair).",
  "baccaratRulesLimits": "Each spot: at least the table minimum. Player and Banker up to the table maximum, tie and pairs up to a fifth of the maximum, a coup's total up to twice the maximum. Betting both Player and Banker is allowed.",
  "baccaratRulesRound": "20 s of betting, then Inès deals the whole coup at once: the cards are already public, the squeeze (the card slowly bent) is only an animation. Settlement at the end of the deal.",
  "baccaratRulesPower": "Commission-free (shop): with a Banker bet, no 5% on a Banker win for one coup.",
  "baccaratRulesRoads": "Shoe trends: Bead Plate (one dot per coup, column by column), Big Road (a new column each time the winner changes, ties as green slashes, pair dots) and Big Eye Boy (red: the Big Road repeats its pattern, blue: it breaks it). They predict nothing: every coup is independent.",
  "baccaratRulesMarkets": "Spectator markets: \"Banker wins?\", \"Tie?\", \"A pair?\" (closed at the deal) and, on simulated tables, \"Banker wins at least 46% of the coups of the shoe?\".",
